{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdUniquenessMeromorphic","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdUniquenessMeromorphic","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdUniquenessFinal","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdHrRatio","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdLociDensity"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdUniquenessMeromorphic.phiBProd_uniqueUpToScalar_of_meromorphic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdUniquenessMeromorphic.phiBProd_uniqueUpToScalar_of_meromorphic","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/PhiBProdUniquenessMeromorphic.lean#L54-L102","doc":"**`phiBProd` uniqueness up to scalar — meromorphic + continuous second solution.** Any\nsubstrate `FaddeevPhi` + `FaddeevPhiStripGrowth` solution `Φ₁` that is meromorphic on `ℂ` and\ncontinuous wherever `phiBProd ≠ 0` is proportional to `phiBProd`. This weakens the normal-form\nhypothesis of `phiBProd_uniqueUpToScalar_of_normalForm` to `MeromorphicOn` + continuity, the\nhonest physical statement. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:faddeev-phib-uniqueness","label":"prop:faddeev-phib-uniqueness","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/faddeev-phib-uniqueness.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdUniquenessMeromorphic.hr_ratio_of_continuous","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdUniquenessMeromorphic.hr_ratio_of_continuous","kind":"theorem","line":36,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/PhiBProdUniquenessMeromorphic.lean#L36-L52","doc":"**`hr_ratio` for a continuous meromorphic second solution.** If `Φ₁` is meromorphic on `ℂ`\nand continuous at `z` (where `phiBProd b z ≠ 0`), then `Φ₁` is analytic at `z`\n(`MeromorphicAt.analyticAt`), so the ratio `Φ₁/phiBProd` is analytic — hence its normal form\nagrees with the pointwise ratio. No normal-form hypothesis on `Φ₁`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}